Night sweep November 8 2026, fired on the 03:00 MST tick six hours after the evening pass, and both named surfaces were quiet in the measurable sense. The mcpservers.org twelve-sitemap canonical returned 20,000 distinct server URLs from 63,359 raw loc entries across the twelve server maps (servers/1 to servers/10, priority-servers, remote-mcp-servers) with the inline locale prefixes stripped case-insensitively before the /servers/ subset was taken as the universe, set identical to the 21:00 capture with zero additions and zero removals, which is now the second consecutive pass to return the same 20,000 and the first pair of passes on this surface to agree at that number. A counting note belongs in the ledger because it cost time: a first attempt stripped locales with [a-z]{2} and read 37,483 URLs, because /pt-BR/ and /zh-CN/ survived a case-sensitive strip and multiplied the count, so the strip has to be case-insensitive and the number has to be reconciled against the raw loc count before it is trusted. The sitemap index now also carries skills.xml beside static.xml and the twelve server maps, so a raw union over every document in the index over-reports by the skills and marketing surface; only the twelve server maps were read, which is the same method as the previous passes. mcpservers.org /all answered 403 again, the standing condition since November 4, so no detail page could be read. The mcp.so homepage was read twice, plain then cache-busted, with identical results: 172 createdAt occurrences, 159 distinct stamps, newest 2026-11-08T08:56:02.072Z, and exactly one card strictly above the evening stamp of 2026-11-08T06:00:20.000Z, which was PendingDelete.net. The submission queue carried the window: twelve numbers from 6923 to 6935 with 6932 absent, read with titles and bodies in one authenticated gh api call, every one an arrival and none a listing update. One server was catalogued. DutyKit at https://dutykit.dev/mcp is remote Streamable HTTP, keyless, protocol 20